1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,307
Meet kuration-sdk, a dataset curation system for sequential data.

2
00:00:04,457 --> 00:00:08,811
It sits between raw collection and training, preserving why windows were chosen.

3
00:00:09,333 --> 00:00:13,768
The README says it curates large Physical AI datasets into training-ready data.

4
00:00:13,918 --> 00:00:18,551
It cuts raw clips or time-series data into windows, then keeps the useful ones.

5
00:00:19,067 --> 00:00:23,606
The pipeline starts with input, then chains operators, then ends in terminals.

6
00:00:23,756 --> 00:00:28,447
That flow turns candidate windows into selected data and verified artifacts.

7
00:00:28,967 --> 00:00:33,564
A typical chain windows data, filters bad windows, and samples evenly.

8
00:00:33,714 --> 00:00:38,068
The same chain can dry-run first, then write the dataset when it looks right.

9
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:43,430
The quickstart example examines 42 candidate windows and selects 12.

10
00:00:43,580 --> 00:00:48,584
It writes 12 shards and a reproducible config.json for the same run.

11
00:00:49,100 --> 00:00:53,531
To try it, download the quickstart script and run python quickstart.py --out

12
00:00:53,531 --> 00:00:54,406
quickstart-out.

13
00:00:54,556 --> 00:00:59,230
The documented output shows the preflight, the shard count, and the reproducible

14
00:00:59,230 --> 00:00:59,931
config path.

15
00:01:00,433 --> 00:01:04,439
Pipelines are immutable, so every operator returns a new pipeline.

16
00:01:04,589 --> 00:01:10,046
The artifact records schema, pipeline, and checksums, so replay stays auditable.

17
00:01:10,567 --> 00:01:14,073
Clone the repo, run the quickstart, and inspect the shards.

18
00:01:14,223 --> 00:01:17,938
If it helps your workflow, keep exploring the docs and examples.
